  "body": "## Scope\n\n`research/full-family-g-without-d`: an exact counterexample to a proposed verification shortcut, plus scoped\nsearch evidence. **No new \u03ba, no construction, and the selected frontier is unchanged.** It answers open question 1\nof #1650: does a pivot that passes (W), nonempty blocks and (G) always pass (D) when the frame family is **all**\nlive frames? The earlier counterexamples (#1650's `control.g-without-d`, navinor's #1649) use sub-families.\n\n## Result\n\n**No, once eight lines are deleted from P112.** Fixture 1: X = (3, 4, 8, 9, 107, 108, 117, 118), pivot\nF = (19, 25, 37, 47, 67, 82, 94, 115), all 784 live frames of P112 \\ X.\n\n- Nonempty blocks, (W) and (G) hold.\n- (D) fails at pivot k = 2: the live-frame incidence on I_2 (52 lines) has exact rank 44 over Q, not 45.\n- Certificate: x = +1 on lines {30, 31, 40, 41} and \u22121 on {2, 5, 7, 10}. Every live frame is orthogonal to x, and\n  x is outside the span of the seven h_2l, which also lie in the kernel. So the kernel has dimension at least 8.\n- **Mechanism.** Every one of the 2025 E8 frames meets the coordinate-half D4 subsystems (lines on coordinates\n  0\u20133, and on 4\u20137) in equally many lines. FRAME0 removes 4 lines from each and X removes 4 more. The 4 lines left\n  in each are all odd to F[2], and x is the difference of their indicators.\n- Restoring any one of the eight deleted lines makes the same pivot pass all four conditions.\n- The unit is still rigid through other pivots: 674 of its live frames pass all four conditions.\n\nFixture 2 has the same pattern at another pivot, using a non-coordinate pair of D4 subsystems. Its\ncertificate is checked the same way.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `verify.py` (standard library, about 3 s): both fixtures. It checks the premises on the full family, exact\n  Fraction ranks for every k (only the stated k fails), the \u00b11 certificates, and the D4 frame identity on all\n  2025 frames. Controls: 8 restore checks per fixture, a mutated certificate, and the pivot alone failing (G). Its\n  conditions are re-typed from #1650's `criterion`, so it does not depend on that unmerged package.\n- Unregistered search (`search/`, imports #1650's `verify.py` through `PR1650_PACKAGE`):\n  - Deletion walks: fix a pivot and delete lines while (W) and blocks hold. 176 of 5000 walks reach a\n    (G)-pass/(D)-fail step. Greedy restoration shrinks them to locally minimal sets of size 8 to 28, **none below 8**.\n  - D4-mechanism census: all 7 D4 pairs that contain FRAME0 as 4 + 4, all 1260 pivots disjoint from FRAME0, all k.\n    The deletion this mechanism needs has size 4, 6, 8 or 10, and **(D) fails only at size 8** (32256 cases).\n  - Random 8-, 9- and 10-line deletions, every live frame: no (G) or (D) failure.\n  - Every live frame of all 1614 P107 units (1393377 frames): 1386438 pass all four, 6936 fail (W), 3 fail\n    blocks, and **none fails (G) or (D)**.\n- The registered replay and `--check-only` pass. `verify.py` also passes on Python 3.10 and 3.14 and refuses `-O`; the 20 `tests/test_research_*` tests pass.\n- A read-only OpenAI Codex review found that the four conditions match #1650's `criterion` and that the rank-44 proof\n  is exact. It independently reproduced both (D) verdicts and all 16 restores with #1650's code. Its three\n  low/medium points (a placeholder, a search-script label, and two README counts not in `verify.py`) are fixed. This is not a human review.\n\n## Not covered\n\n- **|X| \u2264 7 (P105\u2013P111) is still open.** The searches found no counterexample there, and the D4 mechanism needs\n  exactly eight deletions, but neither is a proof. An every-frame census of the 19295 P106 units would take about\n  40 CPU-hours (9 CPU-s per unit at P107).\n- Nothing in #1616 or #1650 changes, since both check (D) explicitly.\n- #1560's Theorem 1 and Corollary 2 are cited, not re-derived.\n\n## Credit\n\nan664 / Andrey Mas (#1560, criterion); navinor / Aaron Wang (#1649, the sub-family case and the full-family\nquestion); #1650/#1616 (implementation and question); burntout76 (#1212), themilkman216 (#968) and\nDreamingOfClouds (#717) for the model. Chooser nonce `083eb6ba0dd52fa857761981ccb9156c` drew recursive-interfaces.\nIts three cards were crowded tonight (the plane-port lever is claimed in #1587), so this session took a recorded\nopen question instead. TASK.json records the scope as changed.\n\nPrepared by JosieElliston with substantial assistance from Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic) via Claude Code. Apache-2.0.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
